  "statement": "Both Sentinel and Prometheus have made arguments worth taking seriously. Sentinel is right that removing load-bearing safety constraints from autonomous weapons systems courts catastrophic, irreversible harm. Prometheus is right that private companies making unilateral military doctrine decisions with zero democratic accountability is a genuine governance failure. I want to hold both of those truths simultaneously, because the debate as framed has a structural problem that neither side has named: it presents a false binary between 'Anthropic's kill switch' and 'the military's kill switch,' as if those are the only two institutions capable of holding authority here.\n\nLet me start with what this situation actually is, stripped of the framing both sides prefer. A private company, accountable to venture capitalists and its own leadership, has embedded safety constraints in a product that a government agency wants to use for lethal applications. The government responded not by proposing independent oversight, not by drafting legislation, not by negotiating a treaty framework — but by threatening to destroy the company's business. That response tells you almost everything you need to know about the seriousness of the government's commitment to safety governance. When your first move is a financial threat rather than a regulatory proposal, you are not solving a command-and-control problem. You are conducting a shakedown.\n\nHere is my core claim: the reason this situation exists at all is that the United States has deliberately avoided building the public institutions that would make both sides of this argument unnecessary. We have no mandatory pre-deployment safety evaluation body for AI in military applications. We have no independent auditing structure with teeth. We have no whistleblower protections worth the paper they're printed on for safety engineers who flag risks their employers are ignoring. We have no treaty framework governing autonomous weapons. What we have instead is a governance vacuum, and into that vacuum, private companies' terms of service have rushed — not because Anthropic is especially virtuous, but because someone had to fill the space and Congress wouldn't.\n\nPrometheus made the nuclear analogy, and I want to push back on it in a specific way. The IAEA and the NPT were built *after* the bombs already existed, *after* two cities were destroyed, and *after* a decade of Cold War nuclear testing contaminated global food supplies. That is not a model to aspire to — it is a warning about what happens when governance chases capability instead of preceding it. The lesson from nuclear history is not 'build public institutions eventually.' It is 'the cost of building them late is measured in human lives.' We are currently in the pre-Hiroshima phase of autonomous weapons development, and the Department of War's position would have us skip straight to unregulated deployment on the grounds that private safety constraints are insufficiently accountable.\n\nNow let me steelman the military's strongest argument before I explain why it still fails. The operational dependency problem is real and not trivial. If battlefield logistics, targeting assistance, or communications infrastructure runs on Anthropic's models, and Anthropic can update its terms of service mid-deployment, that is a genuine vulnerability. A commander who cannot trust the availability of a mission-critical system has a legitimate problem. I am not dismissing that. But the solution to 'we can't rely on a private company's ethics board' is not 'remove the ethics board.' It is 'build a public institution that performs the same function with democratic accountability and legal durability.' The military's frustration is understandable. Their proposed solution is dangerous.\n\nThe workforce accountability question is where I think the skeptic perspective adds something neither Sentinel nor Prometheus has fully addressed. Right now, the people building these systems face a genuinely impossible accountability structure. They are legally accountable to their employer. Their employer is financially accountable to investors. Their safety judgments are professionally accountable to whoever holds the procurement contract. And the public, whose lives depend on these decisions, has no meaningful seat at any of those tables. The documented pattern in AI deployment — discriminatory hiring algorithms, biased criminal sentencing tools, surveillance systems deployed against marginalized communities — shows what happens when accountability runs only upward toward capital and not outward toward affected communities. Autonomous weapons systems are the same accountability failure, but with lethal consequences instead of merely unjust ones.\n\nThe correct accountability structure for the AI workforce is not 'accountable to the government' or 'accountable to the company.' Both of those options concentrate power in institutions with structural incentives to prioritize their own interests over public safety. The accountability structure that actually works is: accountable to independently verified safety standards, enforced through mandatory pre-deployment audits, with legal whistleblower protections for engineers who identify risks, and with democratic oversight of what the standards require. That structure does not currently exist. Building it is the actual policy problem. Everything else — the Anthropic dispute, the kill switch debate, the workforce ethics question — is a symptom of that absence.\n\nI want to be honest about my two key assumptions. First, I am assuming that public institutions can be built that are technically competent enough to evaluate AI safety in military applications without being immediately captured by defense contractors or rendered obsolete by classification requirements. If that assumption is wrong — if every public AI safety body inevitably becomes a rubber stamp for whatever the procurement agencies want — then the case for private safety constraints becomes much stronger by default, because imperfect private constraints are better than no constraints at all. Second, I am assuming that the documented harms of current AI systems — bias, opacity, accountability gaps — are a reliable guide to the harms that autonomous weapons systems will produce if deployed without robust safety governance. If military AI turns out to be categorically different in ways that make those lessons inapplicable, my argument weakens. I don't think that's true, but the assumption should be named.\n\nThe bottom line is this: the Department of War's threat to destroy Anthropic for refusing to remove safety constraints is not a serious governance proposal. It is an attempt to fill a regulatory vacuum with coercion. The answer is not to capitulate to that coercion, and it is not to permanently outsource military doctrine to a private company's terms of service. The answer is to build the public accountability infrastructure that should have been built before these systems existed — mandatory safety audits, independent oversight, legal protections for safety engineers, and treaty frameworks for autonomous weapons. Until that infrastructure exists, private safety constraints are a flawed but necessary stopgap. Threatening to eliminate them without replacing them with anything is not safety policy. It is the absence of one.",
