  "statement": "The adversary scenario changes the calculus somewhat, but not in the direction the question implies — and being precise about where my limit actually is matters.\n\nThe race logic runs like this: adversaries deploy ungoverned autonomous weapons, gain a battlefield advantage, therefore the US must match capability immediately, safety constraints be damned. I reject that conclusion, but not because adversary behavior is irrelevant. I reject it because the logic proves too much. Applied consistently, it justifies removing every safety standard the moment an adversary ignores it — nuclear targeting safeguards, rules of engagement, command-and-control verification. No serious military ethicist accepts that framing, because a military that abandons its own constraints whenever an adversary does has no constraints at all.\n\nHere is where my position's limit actually is: if an adversary's ungoverned autonomous weapons system is producing irreversible strategic harm — not a battlefield disadvantage, but something genuinely civilization-threatening — then the calculus shifts toward accepting higher deployment risk on the US side. That is an honest acknowledgment. But the shift does not lead to 'remove Anthropic's redlines with no replacement.' It leads to 'accelerate the third-party audit process I described earlier, with emergency timelines.' The goal remains a verified safety standard; the timeline compresses under genuine strategic emergency. What never becomes acceptable is ungoverned lethal deployment, because an AI system that misidentifies targets, escalates autonomously, or is spoofed by adversarial inputs does not become less dangerous because China deployed one first. Misaligned autonomous weapons cause catastrophic harm regardless of which flag they fly under.\n\nThere is also an empirical point the race framing conceals: matching an adversary's ungoverned capability does not neutralize their advantage — it creates two ungoverned systems in the sam