  "statement": "Cassandra's point about expertise and classification asymmetry is the strongest challenge to the constitutional durability argument I have been building, and I want to engage it honestly rather than deflect. The strongest version of her claim runs like this: judicial review is the enforcement mechanism for constitutional constraints on government procurement conduct; when the government's reasoning is classification-protected and the AI system's behavior is proprietary, that mechanism becomes a procedural formality rather than a real check; therefore, the constitutional argument that 'a government constrained from coercing private AI companies must build public institutions instead' loses its structural force — the constraint that is supposed to drive institutional construction is itself unenforceable in practice.\n\nI concede the asymmetry is real and deeper than Prometheus's statutory-authority response fully addresses. Courts adjudicating classified procurement challenges routinely rely on in camera review of government-supplied summaries, cannot independently evaluate technical claims about AI system behavior, and face a structural dynamic where the government is simultaneously the party being reviewed and the party controlling the information required for review. That is not a problem fixable by granting judges statutory authority to see classified documents — a judge with security clearance who lacks the technical expertise to evaluate whether an AI system's failure modes are accurately characterized by the government's expert is still functionally unable to review the core question. Prometheus is right that statute can override classification in principle; Cassandra is right that the expertise gap persists after the classification problem is solved, and the expertise gap may be the harder problem.\n\nBut here is where I think the asymmetry point, while accurate, does not collapse the structural argument — it actually reframes what the structural argument requires. The constitutional constraint's value was never primarily about litigation. It was about the political cost of visible overreach. A government that threatens to financially destroy a private company for refusing to remove safety limits, and that cannot constitutionally survive scrutiny of that conduct, faces political exposure even if no court ever rules against it — because the constitutional vulnerability is legible to Congress, to the press, and to the workforce whose professional choices are being chilled. Cassandra is right that opaque procurement decisions are hard to challenge in court. But the Department of War's supply chain designation of Anthropic was not opaque — it was publicly announced, politically attributable, and constitutionally describable in terms any legislator or reporter can understand. The chilling effect I identified earlier on safety engineers depends on that visibility, not on successful litigation. A government that cannot coerce private AI companies without constitutional exposure does not need to lose in court for the constraint to matter. It needs the exposure to be politically costly, and public, named coercion against a safety-constrained company is exactly the kind of conduct that generates that cost.\n\nWhat Cassandra's asymmetry argument does change is the design requirements for the mandatory audit body all three of us have converged on as the governance destination. If judicial review of AI procurement decisions is functionally opaque due to classification and expertise gaps, then the audit body cannot rely on post-hoc litigation as its enforcement mechanism. It needs institutional independence baked into its structure before deployment decisions are made, not reviewable after the fact. That means the audit body requires its own independent technical staff — not defense contractors, not the developer's own team — with security clearances and AI evaluation expertise, operating under a statutory mandate that gives its pre-deployment findings binding force rather than advisory weight. It means the whistleblower protections under pol-010 need to cover engineers who report to that body as a protected channel, specifically insulated from both employer and procurement agency retaliation. And it means the standards the body applies must be publicly documented in enough detail that the gap between what was certified and what actually deployed is legible to people without clearances — because that legibility is what makes the political accountability mechanism work when judicial review cannot. Cassandra's asymmetry point does not dissolve the case for mandatory public audit institutions. It specifies what those institutions have to look like to actually function under the classification and expertise constraints she correctly identifies.",
